{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Are they not.</b> He gave them a sign." "<b>Behind.</b> Far across the Jordan, onward to a distance. And this is the meaning of אַחֲרֵי — wherever אַחֲרֵי is said, it denotes remoteness." "<b>The way of the going down of the sun.</b> Westward from the Jordan. And the sense of Scripture proves that they are two things, for they are punctuated with two te'amim: 'אַחֲרֵי' has a pashta, and 'דֶרֶךְ' has a munach, and it has a dagesh. And if 'אַחֲרֵי דֶרֶךְ' were one phrase, then 'אַחֲרֵי' would have a mesharet with a reversed shofar, and 'דֶרֶךְ' would have a pashta and a rafeh." "<b>Over against the Gilgal.</b> Far from the Gilgal." "<b>The oaks of Moreh.</b> That is Shechem, as it is said (Genesis 12) ״עַד מְקוֹם שְׁכֶם עַד אֵלוֹן מוֹרֶה״ (Sifrei)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {2 "Rashi's description of the cantillation assumes 'אַחֲרֵי' carries a conjunctive (mesharet) preceding 'דֶרֶךְ' with a disjunctive (pashta) if the two words formed one phrase; 'שופר הפוך' (inverted shofar) is a specific cantillation term. The exact te'amim tradition reflected here may differ from the standard Tiberian system."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:46:43.314330873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}